        "title": "Watchmaking - 02 The Verge Escapement",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The escapement is the clock's fundamental innovation. Without it, a falling weight drives a gear train at whatever rate friction allows — not useful for counting time. The escapement converts continuous rotational energy into a controlled stepping motion, and it counts those steps against an oscillator."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-VERGE-ESCAPEMENT.md#004",
      "path": [
        "02-VERGE-ESCAPEMENT.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:watchmaking:02-verge-escapement, git-history:watchmaking:02-verge-escapement]",
        "canonical_path": "watchmaking/02-VERGE-ESCAPEMENT.md",
        "concepts": "[verge, escapement]",
        "current_path": "watchmaking/02-VERGE-ESCAPEMENT.md",
        "id": "maxim:watchmaking:verge-escapement",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "watchmaking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[verge, escapement]",
        "section": "watchmaking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Watchmaking - 02 The Verge Escapement",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The verge-and-foliot is the first mechanical escapement. It appeared in European tower clocks in the 1280s. It was good enough to ring bells at canonical hours. It was not good enough to keep accurate time. Why not: it had an escapement but no proper oscillator. The foliot (a horizontal bar) has no natural period — it simply oscillates at whatever rate the gear train drives it. Remove the driving force and it stops immediately."
    },
    {
